<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.App</RootNamespace>
        <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        <Description>
            The host an application boots through: VixenApp.Run&lt;TGame&gt;(args) picks a platform,
            opens a window, wires the services and owns the frame loop — and every step of it is a
            documented sequence the application can inline and edit instead.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>host;bootstrap;game-loop;app;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      What doc 02 always described this as: "a sensible default reference set for an app head". The
      host itself is Core/Vixen.App.Hosting, under the RUNTIME profile where frame code belongs;
      three files are left here, and all three are here because they name an implementation.

        * GraphicsHost   — Vulkan where there is a surface to present to, Null where there is not.
        * PlatformHost   — the desktop platform, falling back to headless.
        * VixenApp       — the entry point, and the only place those two are installed as defaults.

      ⚠ A head that wants a different backend does not edit this. It hands one to
      AppBuilder.WithGraphics / WithPlatform, or an IGraphicsBackend / IPlatformFactory of its own
      to WithGraphicsBackend / WithPlatformFactory — which is what those interfaces exist for, and
      what makes them live in Core rather than here.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.App.Hosting\Vixen.App.Hosting.csproj" />

        <!--
          The contracts the two selectors below speak in: IPlatform, IWindow, and the device and
          swapchain interfaces. Named rather than taken transitively, because this assembly's whole
          job is to return one of each.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Platform\Vixen.Platform.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics\Vixen.Graphics.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the four implementations an app head can boot on its own. Doc 02 describes Vixen.App
          as the package that pulls in the backends valid for a RID, and this is that: Vulkan and
          the desktop platform where there is a display, Null and headless where there is not —
          which is doc 17's dedicated server, running the same frame shape against a device that
          draws nothing.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan\Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Graphics.Null\Vixen.Graphics.Null.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the two a head can ask for by name. Neither is in GraphicsHost.Default — WebGPU opens
          only where Dawn or wgpu-native is installed, and OpenGL cannot be opened by an app head at
          all yet (no Vixen.Platform implementation creates a GL context). They are referenced so
          that asking for them is a reported refusal rather than a missing type, which is what makes
          the gap visible from a log instead of from reading a csproj.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Graphics.WebGPU\Vixen.Graphics.WebGPU.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Graphics.OpenGL\Vixen.Graphics.OpenGL.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Platform.Desktop\Vixen.Platform.Desktop.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform\Vixen.Platform.Headless\Vixen.Platform.Headless.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.App.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
